= Your Daily Curiosity Drill

Scientific literacy compounds the way GK does — small daily exposures, noted down. The drill takes ten minutes and needs nothing but the world outside your window. It builds the exact muscle this section tests: seeing a phenomenon and producing its mechanism in plain words.

#table(
  columns: (1.05fr, 2.5fr, 1.95fr),
  table.header[*Minute*][*The drill*][*The standard to reach*],
  [1-3], [Pick ONE everyday phenomenon you met today (steam, a shadow, a rusting nail) and say its mechanism aloud], [A because-sentence with HOW inside it],
  [4-6], [Test one claim you meet online against the five-item checklist], [Verdict: evidence, mechanism, source, alternatives, framing — named one by one],
  [7-8], [Spot one correlation in the news and hunt the hidden third factor], [A named third cause, or an honest "none visible yet"],
  [9-10], [Redraw one mechanism from this PDF from memory, then check against the book], [Ten mechanism cards owned, not read],
)

- *Keep a mechanism notebook:* one line per phenomenon — the sight, then the because. Twenty lines a week is a complete revision deck.
- *Re-read the notebook, not textbooks, the night before the exam* — your own collected mechanisms are the highest-value page you own.

